00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.549 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  my name is macy and i am a jeweler at  beijing vintage  i am going to walk you through how to  restore a 1930s art deco ring  this ring is a 14 karat yellow gold  setting the gemstone in this ring is a  created spinel stone  the biggest things wrong with this ring  were the misshapen band there's a  resizing bar at the bottom of the ring  band from a previous resizing and then  the gemstone will need to be replaced if  it's not in any salvageable condition  this ring  might probably only be worth maybe a  hundred dollars and when we're finished  this ring might sell for about four to  five thousand dollars  [Music]  the first thing that i will do with any  restoration is to take out any gemstones  that can't be saved there's a large part  on the underside of the stone that had  cracked off  i'm getting rid of the resizing band  because oftentimes especially in vintage  and antique pieces the resizing was  often poorly done  this tool is called a jeweler saw and  i'm using this to cut out the sizing bar  rather than unsoldering it just because  i was having some trouble on soldering  it so just to be safe and  eliminate the chance of accidentally  melting the whole ring while trying to  unsolder it i just decided to cut it out  soldering is a process of joining two  pieces of metal with solder and that  solder can be either silver solder or  gold solder  when i remove that piece of metal  solder will only flow if it's a perfect  seam so once i  cut the sizing bar out i have to file  the ends so they're perfectly parallel  to each other so that when i close that  seam there's no  air in between so that the solder can  flow  i am hammering the seam shut so i'm  trying to  close those two ends together even if  the ring band isn't perfectly round that  can always be fixed later as long as  those two ends are touching each other  to get them ready to be soldered back  together and then i take the ring to my  soldering bench and i'm going to prep  the ring for soldering  i apply what is called flux and flux  will prevent any oxides that will form  on the surface of the ring while being  soldered from the torch from building up  and then adding my gold solder to the  seam where i intend to have it flow then  i'm going to be using a 14 karat gold  solder which is essentially just an  alloy of gold and also zinc and zinc is  what makes the metal  melt at a low temperature so it allows  the solder to flow into the seam of what  i'm trying to connect and it will bind  those two pieces together permanently  and then i use my torch to evenly heat  up the piece in order to get the solder  up to  its melting temperature  the prongs on this ring are cracked and  worn from age they just need a little  bit of reinforcement i add some solder  to the prongs to give them some extra  thickness so i can create a new seat  later on in the process for the stone  once i'm finished with the prongs i take  the ring back to my bench and remove  some of the extra solder material from  the inside of the ring band i use  a tool called a flex shaft with a  silicone bit and that's going to remove  some of the material from the solder  seam so that when i hammer it round  again the solder doesn't  embed itself into the metal  once that material is mostly removed i  move on to hammering a  round shape back into the ring with a  rawhide mallet and a steel ring mandrel  and since the steel ring mandrel is  tapered to accommodate for all different  kinds of sizes of rings i  frequently flip back and forth and work  from both sides to make sure that one  side is not  larger than the other side and i work  from all sides of the piece except for  on the ring head because i don't want to  deform the head of the ring  once i'm done  rounding the band back out i go ahead  and i start removing some of the solder  from the outside of the band and i use  this with a hand file and then once i'm  finished with a hand file i start using  some silicone  polishing wheels with my boredom flex  shaft to further remove some of the  scratches and begin the process of  starting to polish the piece i go  through a process of four different  abrasivenesses starting from the most  coarse to the finest to get the bulk of  the polishing done on the outside of the  beef  once i'm done working on the ring band i  move on to polishing the inside of the  ring starting with a polishing compound  that's called white diamond and this is  an abrasive polishing compound so even  though it's polishing it still removes  material from the surface of the metal  and then after that i move on to a  polishing compound called fabuluster  which is a polishing compound that is  very tiny clay bits so it kind of brings  out the really luster high polish finish  to the metal  and once i'm done with that i repeat the  entire process on the outside of the  ring so that i can  get the ring back to a mirror finish  like it should be  [Music]  once i'm done with polishing  i move on to getting the stone ready to  be set  [Music]  i'm replacing the created spinel with a  diamond because it's a large enough  setting that it would make a really nice  engagement ring it's a solid ring  setting  this diamond is 1.3 carats  to prepare for the diamond to be set i  kind of evaluate where all the prongs  are at i make sure that they're all even  they're all nice and reinforced from the  stuttering that i have already done  and i start to carve out  the material with  some setting burrs and bulburs to try  and create a more perfect seat for the  diamond to sit in  once that's done i use a little bit of  wax to place the stone  into the ring setting  and from there this is a good fit i'm  ready to start pushing the prongs over  and so i  use a tool that is called a bezel pusher  to start to push over the prongs to hold  the diamond in place  [Music]  we are so happy with how this ring came  out  and we're so happy to give it a second  life  [Music]  you

